  PERCENTAGE
}

// The three GST registration types a jewellery business can operate under
// in India - see BusinessSettings.gstScheme. Each has real legal
// consequences for what an invoice/quotation/purchase bill must show:
//   REGULAR_B2C  - Retailer. Full SGST+CGST (or IGST if inter-state) shown
//                  and collected from the customer. Customer GSTIN optional.
//   REGULAR_B2B  - Wholesaler & Manufacturer. Same tax collection as B2C,
//                  but selling to other GST-registered businesses, so the
//                  buyer's GSTIN is required for a valid B2B tax invoice.
//   COMPOSITION  - Small local jeweler under the Composition Scheme. Legally
//                  PROHIBITED from showing/collecting GST as a line item on
//                  any document - no SGST/CGST/IGST columns at all. Must
//                  print "Bill of Supply" (never "Tax Invoice") with the
//                  mandatory disclaimer. The dealer still pays a small flat
//                  tax to the government, but out of their own margin, never
//                  itemized to the customer, and can never claim input tax
//                  credit on purchases either.
enum GstScheme {
  REGULAR_B2C
  REGULAR_B2B
  COMPOSITION
}

// A Customer's or Vendor's OWN GST registration status - independent of
// BusinessSettings.gstScheme above, which only describes the STORE's own
// registration. B2B vs B2C is not a registration category a counterparty
// can "be" - it's simply whether they're GST-registered at all, which is
// exactly what this models:
//   UNREGISTERED - No GSTIN. GSTIN is never required/shown for this party.
//   REGULAR      - Has a GSTIN, charges/receives full GST normally. As a
//                  Vendor, their invoice to us legitimately shows GST - see
//                  gstinRequired()/isVendorGstApplicable() in lib/gst.ts.
//   COMPOSITION  - Has a GSTIN, but (same legal restriction as GstScheme's
//                  own COMPOSITION) can never itemize GST on an invoice THEY
//                  issue. Only meaningful for Vendor: a Composition vendor's
//                  purchase bill to us never carries a tax breakdown,
//                  regardless of what GstScheme our own store operates
//                  under. For a Customer this only affects GSTIN capture,
//                  same as REGULAR - we're still the ones issuing the
//                  invoice, so it's our own GstScheme, not theirs, that
//                  decides what our invoice can show.
enum PartyGstType {
  UNREGISTERED
  REGULAR
  COMPOSITION
}

